<title>POLAND: Poland wants to sell PLN 18.9 bln bonds in 1997.</title>
<headline>Poland wants to sell PLN 18.9 bln bonds in 1997.</headline>
<dateline>WARSAW 1996-12-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Polish finance ministry wants to sell 18.9 billion zlotys worth of bonds on the domestic market next year, down from 28.4 billion sold this year, the head of the public debt department said on Friday.</p>
<p>Piotr Dziewulski told a news conference the ministry had no immediate plans of introducing new instruments next year but would offer all current papers. @</p>
<p> BONDS TO BE OFFERED IN 1997	     AMOUNT IN PLN BLN</p>
<p> index-linked one-year for the public	   1.50</p>
<p> index-linked one-year for institutions	 0.80</p>
<p> fixed interest two-year			    5.10</p>
<p> floating three-year				  3.15</p>
<p> fixed interest five-year			   4.15</p>
<p> floating 10-year				     4.20</p>
<p>NOTE. The planned bond sales for 1997 are in nominal terms.</p>
<p>-- Jan Strupczewski +48 22 653 9700</p>
</text>
